  "query_preview": "..\n(Today through next Monday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: \n\nOne more afternoon and evening of unrestricted monsoonal flow \nwill continue to bring shower and thunderstorm chances for the \nSilver State, but High pressure shifting west from the plains \nlooks to first limit, then cutoff moisture flow. Monday afternoon \nand evening there will again be a 20% to 50% chance of isolated to\nscattered showers and thunderstorms, but these chances drop to \n10% to 20% Tuesday, then rise a bit to 10% to 30% Wednesday \nafternoon and evening. Storm chances look to drop below 10% \nThursday into the weekend, the main reason will be the subtropical\nhigh pressure ridge now over the southern plains builds west into\nArizona. Storm modes through Monday still favor more wet storm \nmodes, but there will still be a chance for a few dry \nthunderstorms, both as storm motion increases due to increasing \nsouthwesterly flow and from mid level moisture decreasing. Tuesday\nand Wednesday chances for dry thunder will increase, but storm \ncoverage will shift east as drier air returns from the west. \nCommon hazards for storms will include gusty outflow winds up to \n50 MPH, ground lightning strikes, and brief heavy rainfall. \nThursday through Monday, models may have finally settled on a \npattern change. Both the GFS and ECMWF still show that the upper \nridge will try and work its way back west, shifting the upper flow\nback to a WSW direction as it does so. What is interesting is \nmodels are now showing that the ridge will stay farther south over\nthe US/Mex border allowing for a longwave trough to set up \noffshore of the Pacific northwest. This will bring a more westerly\nmid and upper level flow, which would be dry, but it would also \nbe cooler given the lower forecast heights. Still this is a just a\nrecent agreement from the models, so overall confidence remains \nmixed for the extended until this consensus holds firm. \nTemperatures even with the pattern change look to hold in the low \n80s to low 90s, with overnight lows ranging into the mid 50s to \nmid 60s. Winds will be breezy through the weekend and into the new\nweek as winds shift more south to southwest in line with the \nupper level flow pattern at speeds of 10 to 20 MPH with gusts up \nto 30 MPH possible. Winds look to shift back to the west to SW \nThursday through the weekend. \n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: \n \nThere is high confidence a daily 20% to 50% chance for isolated \nto scattered showers and thunderstorms for Nevada through Monday. \n\nThere is moderate confidence in a 10% to 30 % chance for a mix of\nisolated dry and wet thunderstorms Tuesday and Wednesday.\n\nThere is high confidence in dry conditions returning to the Great\nBasin Thursday trough Sunday of next week.\n\nThere is low-moderate confidence in the upper pattern Thursday \nthrough Monday as models now seem to agree on keeping NV under WSW\nflow between as upper level ridge over Arizona, and a trough off \nthe Pacific NW.",
